<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_unwind.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-unwind">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-unwind">
  <primary>UNWIND</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>UNWIND</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>UNWIND</refname>
  <refpurpose>turn a list into rows, one row per element</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
UNWIND <replaceable class="parameter">list_expression</replaceable> AS <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-unwind-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>UNWIND</command> expands a list into the query. For each element it emits one row
   and binds <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> to that element. Everything
   else in scope is carried along unchanged. It is the inverse of
   <literal>collect()</literal>, and the way to get from one row holding a list to many rows
   holding its parts.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>AS <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></literal> is required.
   There is no unnamed form.
  </para>

  <para>
   The list may be a literal, a property holding a list, a variable that
   <literal>collect()</literal> produced, or any expression that yields a jsonb array or a
   PostgreSQL array. A scalar is refused. An empty list produces no rows, so the input row
   disappears with it. A null inside the list is an element like any other and produces a row.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>UNWIND</command> runs once per incoming row and the rows it produces multiply out.
   Three input rows over a two element list give six. Nest two
   <command>UNWIND</command> clauses to flatten a list of lists.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>UNWIND</command> may open a query or follow another clause. It cannot end one.
  </para>

  <para>
   <xref linkend="cypher-for"/> is the GQL counterpart and does the same job, with an optional
   ordinal.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-unwind-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">list_expression</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An expression yielding a jsonb array or a PostgreSQL array. See
      <xref linkend="cypher-expr"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name bound to each element. It is visible to every clause after this one.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-unwind-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Expand a list literal. Six rows, in list order, duplicates included:
<programlisting>
UNWIND [1, 91, 203, 1, 7, 91] AS n
RETURN n;

  n
-----
 1
 91
 203
 1
 7
 91
(6 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Drive a <command>MATCH</command> from a list. This is the usual way to run a query over a
   list of keys supplied by the client, with the list passed as one parameter:
<programlisting>
UNWIND ['The Matrix', 'Inception'] AS t
MATCH (m:movie {title: t})
RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year;

     film     | year
--------------+------
 "The Matrix" | 1999
 "Inception"  | 2010
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A property may hold a list, and <command>UNWIND</command> opens it:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
UNWIND m.embedding AS component
RETURN component;

  component
-------------
 -0.07594558
 0.04081754
 0.29592122
 -0.11921061
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>collect()</literal> folds rows into a list and <command>UNWIND</command> unfolds
   it again. The pair is how you aggregate and then go back to rows:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
WITH collect(DISTINCT m.genre) AS genres
UNWIND genres AS genre
RETURN genre
ORDER BY genre;

    genre
-------------
 "Action"
 "Adventure"
 "Sci-Fi"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Flatten a list of lists with two clauses, the second over the variable the first bound:
<programlisting>
UNWIND [[1, 2], [3]] AS pair
UNWIND pair AS x
RETURN pair, x;

  pair  | x
--------+---
 [1, 2] | 1
 [1, 2] | 2
 [3]    | 3
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An empty list produces no rows, and the input row goes with them. Use
   <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> or a <literal>CASE</literal> guard when a row has to
   survive an empty list:
<programlisting>
UNWIND [] AS x RETURN x;

 x
---
(0 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A null element is still an element. It prints as <literal>null</literal> because the output
   column is jsonb, and a jsonb null is a value rather than a missing one:
<programlisting>
UNWIND [1, NULL, 3] AS x RETURN x;

  x
------
 1
 null
 3
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-unwind-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>UNWIND</command> requires a list. Neither a scalar nor a bare
   <literal>NULL</literal> is treated as a list of one element or of none:
<programlisting>
UNWIND 3 AS x RETURN x;

ERROR:  expression must be jsonb or array, but integer
LINE 1: UNWIND 3 AS x RETURN x;
               ^
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
UNWIND NULL AS x RETURN x;

ERROR:  expression must be jsonb or array, but unknown
LINE 1: UNWIND NULL AS x RETURN x;
               ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Wrap a value that might not be a list, as in
   <literal>UNWIND coalesce(m.tags, []) AS tag</literal>, when the property is not guaranteed
   to be there.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-unwind-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no <command>UNWIND</command>. Its list expanding statement is the
   FOR statement of section 14.8, which AgensGraph also implements, as
   <xref linkend="cypher-for"/>. <command>FOR</command> adds a
   <literal>WITH OFFSET</literal> clause that gives each element its position counting from 0,
   which <command>UNWIND</command> cannot do. The two are otherwise interchangeable.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>UNWIND</command> is the openCypher spelling and behaves as it does there. The list
   may additionally be a PostgreSQL array here, not only a jsonb array.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

